#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Prints a short story about the end of times.

cat <<'EOF'
The Last Hourglass

The sky did not fall all at once. First the clocks forgot how to agree—church bells striking thirteen while wristwatches showed noon, subway schedules printed in languages no one had spoken for centuries. Astronomers called it drift; poets called it the long exhale of something vast waking up.

In the observatory on Mount Veridian, Dr. Elara Chen watched the stars slide sideways like wet paint. Her graduate student, Marco, had stopped filling the logbook three nights ago and instead wrote letters to people who would never read them. "If the end is a story," he told her, "someone ought to leave a margin note for whoever turns the page next."

The oceans did not boil. They simply grew quiet. Whales surfaced once, sang one chord that cracked every window for a hundred miles, and dove into depths that no longer had a bottom on any chart. Cities kept their lights on out of habit. Coffee shops still opened at six. A barista in Oslo served the last cappuccino anyone would ever order and drew a small spiral in the foam because spirals were what her grandmother taught her when words failed.

Elara broadcast on every frequency she could hijack—not warnings, but readings: the temperature of the void between galaxies, the half-life of hope measured in decibels, the exact shade of orange the horizon turned when time began to fray. Listeners reported the same dream: standing in a field of ash that smelled like rain on hot pavement, holding a match that would not light, knowing the match was not the point.

On the seventh evening of the seventh month of the year nobody could name, the sun set twice. Between the two sunsets, every person alive heard the same sentence, not in ears but in bone: You were the interval. Make it count.

Marco walked to the cliff edge with his bundle of unsent letters. Elara joined him, carrying the broken hourglass she had kept since childhood—sand long gone, glass cracked, still beautiful. They did not jump. They read the letters aloud to the listening dark: apologies, recipes, instructions for fixing a bicycle, one joke so stupid it made the second sunset pause, as if the universe needed to laugh before closing shop.

When the final grain of light drained from the sky, the hourglass in Elara's hands filled from nothing. One grain. It fell, and in the falling, every clock in the world ticked once, together.

What happened next is disputed by no one, because no one remained to dispute. Some say the interval folded into a seed. Some say the seed was always us. Travelers in dreams still find a cliff, two figures, and an empty cup of coffee cooling on a stone—proof that the end of times was not an erasure but a period at the end of a sentence someone, somewhere, is still writing.

The end.

EOF
